    "description": "Iran’s ICT Research Institute, also identified in institutional material as the Iran Telecommunication Research Center, works across information and communication technology research and policy. International Telecommunication Union documents provide examples involving digital accessibility, child online protection, and contributions to technical standards discussions.",
    "tags": ["ICT Research Institute", "ITRC", "Telecommunications", "Digital accessibility", "Technical standards", "Online safety"],
    "status": null,
    "editorial_status": "needs_research",
    "as_of_date": "2026-10-26",
    "created_at": "2026-10-27T17:33:36Z",
    "updated_at": "2026-10-27T17:33:36Z",
    "seo": {
      "title": "ICT Research Institute: Telecommunications, Accessibility and Standards | Iran, Unfolded",
      "description": "Discover documented examples of Iran’s ICT Research Institute work in telecommunications research, digital accessibility, online safety, and standards proposals."
    },
    "content": [
      {
        "id": "identity",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "Understanding the institutional name",
        "content": "ICT means information and communication technology: the systems used to process information and communicate through digital networks. An institute presentation hosted by the International Telecommunication Union, or ITU, explicitly identifies the ICT Research Institute as also known as the Iran Telecommunication Research Center, abbreviated ITRC. Readers may therefore encounter both names when following its work. [s1]\n\nThe ITU is the United Nations specialized agency concerned with telecommunications and digital technologies. Its records provide several specific examples of the institute’s activities, although they do not replace an institutional charter or a current organizational directory.",
        "source_ids": ["s1"]
      },
      {
        "id": "research-fields",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "A portfolio extending beyond network infrastructure",
        "content": "An ITU Asia-Pacific Centres of Excellence meeting report records a presentation by the institute’s education and international collaboration representative. The listed research areas included artificial intelligence, fifth-generation mobile communications, large-scale data analysis, cloud computing, connected devices, assistive technologies, and software-defined networking. The last of these concerns separating network control from the equipment that forwards traffic, allowing network behavior to be managed through software. [s2]\n\nThe representative offered potential cooperation through training courses, workshops, joint projects, and exchanges of experts. The report establishes the subjects presented and the cooperation offered; it does not show that every proposed activity was subsequently delivered. Nor does a broad portfolio demonstrate equal research capacity or commercial achievement in every field. [s2]",
        "source_ids": ["s2"]
      },
      {
        "id": "accessibility",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "Digital accessibility as a research subject",
        "content": "The ITU’s 2021 report on access to telecommunications and ICT services for persons with disabilities describes an institute study of relevant services and e-health businesses in Iran. It also records awareness workshops involving universities, research institutes, public and non-governmental organizations, and businesses. Their subjects included accessible services and assistive hardware and software. [s3]\n\nThis work broadens the meaning of communications research: a connection is useful only if people can operate the associated devices and services. The ITU report documents research and awareness activities, however, rather than a nationwide measurement of accessibility improvements. It should not be read as proof that barriers were removed throughout Iran. [s3]",
        "source_ids": ["s3"]
      },
      {
        "id": "online-safety",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "Children’s online environment",
        "content": "A presentation hosted in the ITU’s 2018 event materials outlines an Iranian child-online-protection framework. It discusses digital literacy, privacy risks, content and service provision, and coordination among participants in children’s online environment. This is evidence of a policy framework presented by the institute, not an independent assessment of its implementation or effectiveness. Its older connectivity claims are not used here as current statistics. [s1]",
        "source_ids": ["s1"]
      },
      {
        "id": "standards",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "Participation in standards discussions",
        "content": "A public ITU record dated 29 September 2026 identifies the ICT Research Institute as the source of a contribution concerning artificial-intelligence methods for evaluating radio-frequency electromagnetic fields near fifth-generation mobile base stations. The record labels it a proposed justification for a draft technical report. It therefore demonstrates a submitted proposal, not an adopted standard, a validated method, or a finding about health effects. [s4]\n\nThe contribution’s full document requires restricted ITU access and was not inspected. Its public title and abstract establish the topic and submitting institution, but cannot support detailed claims about the proposed algorithm or its performance. Together, these examples show why institutional work should be assessed at the level of identifiable studies, training activities, and proposals, with outcomes verified separately. [s4]",
        "source_ids": ["s4"]
      }
    ],
